# Methodology and findings

This is the reference write-up: the task, the data, the models, how the out-of-sample protocol is enforced, the results, and how far they can be trusted. The short version is the [README](../README.md). A chart-led version is [`notebooks/walkthrough.ipynb`](../notebooks/walkthrough.ipynb).

Every number below is filled in from `data/processed/*.json` by `src/render_docs.py`, so this file cannot disagree with the pipeline's outputs. "We" is the competition entry (Aakash Anil and Stefan von der Heyden); "I" is the work done afterwards.

**Summary.** Over {{n_origins}} Friday origins the combination scored an RMSE of {{rmse_combo}} against {{rmse_ar1_log}} for the mandated AR(1), {{gain_vs_ar1}} per cent lower. The difference is not statistically established (Diebold-Mariano p = {{p_combo_ar1}}; 95% interval for the mean squared-error difference {{ci_combo_ar1_lo}} to {{ci_combo_ar1_hi}}). Against the random walk (RMSE {{rmse_rw}}) the combination is level (p = {{p_combo_rw}}). An equal-weight average of the same three components scored {{rmse_equal3}}, so adaptive weighting adds nothing measurable. All 2021 to 2025 results are exploratory (section 13).

