## 6. The forecast combination

The combination is an inverse discounted-MSE weighted average of the random walk, carry and ridge. At each origin, for each component `m`, take the discounted mean squared error over the last 156 resolved origins:

$$\text{DMSE}_m = \frac{\sum_{k} \delta^{k}\, e_{m,k}^2}{\sum_{k} \delta^{k}}, \qquad \delta = {{chosen_delta}}$$

where `k` runs backwards from the most recent resolved origin. Then weight inversely, with sharpness `θ`:

$$w_m = \frac{\left(1/\text{DMSE}_m\right)^{\theta}}{\sum_j \left(1/\text{DMSE}_j\right)^{\theta}}, \qquad \theta = {{chosen_theta}}$$

With fewer than 26 resolved errors the weights are equal. The weights are non-negative and sum to one, so the combination cannot leave the range of its members.

### What the weights did

They stayed close to equal. Across all {{n_origins}} origins every weight lay between {{w_min}} and {{w_max}}, and the averages were {{w_mean_rw}} (random walk), {{w_mean_carry}} (carry) and {{w_mean_ridge}} (ridge).

{{table_weights_by_year}}

The reason is in the errors. The component forecasts all sit within a fraction of a per cent of spot, so their errors are almost the same series. The largest pairwise correlation among the components' errors, between the {{max_err_corr_pair}}, is {{max_err_corr}}. When the errors are that similar, inverse-error weights barely move off one third each.

### Ablations

Three variants test what the adaptive scheme contributes. `EQUAL3` averages the three components at one third each. `RW_CARRY` and `RW_RIDGE` apply the same adaptive weighting with one component dropped.

`EQUAL3` scored {{rmse_equal3}} against {{rmse_combo}} for the adaptive combination, a difference of {{equal3_vs_combo_gap}} ({{equal3_vs_combo_word}} for equal weights). `RW_CARRY` scored {{rmse_rw_carry}} and `RW_RIDGE` {{rmse_rw_ridge}}. The largest gap between the combination and any of the three is {{ablation_max_gap}}, against a 95% interval half-width of about {{rmse_ci_half_rw}} rand for the combination against the random walk (section 10). I did not test the ablation differences formally, but they are far inside that width. Adaptive weighting is not a demonstrated improvement on equal weighting in this sample.

