## 9. What the AR(1) does differently

An AR(1) with persistence `φ` below one is pulled towards a long-run level. For the log series the level it is pulled towards is

$$\mu = \exp\left(\frac{c}{1-\phi}\right)$$

which is the equilibrium of the log, so it is a median-type level and not the mean of the rate. It is fragile near a unit root. Across the {{n_origins}} origins the estimated `φ` ran from {{phi_min}} to {{phi_max}}, so `1 - φ` ran from {{one_minus_phi_min}} to {{one_minus_phi_max}}. A small change in `c` or `φ` moves `μ` a long way, and it did: `μ` ranged from R{{mu_min}} to R{{mu_max}} as the estimation sample grew, while spot at the origins ran from R{{spot_first}} at the first to R{{spot_peak}} at the highest. At the worked-example origin the fit had {{ex_ar1_n}} observations, `φ` = {{ex_ar1_phi}} and `μ` = R{{ex_ar1_mu}}, against a spot rate of R{{ex_spot_short}}, with a shock half-life of {{ex_half_life}} weeks.

### What the data show

- `μ` was below spot at {{n_below_spot}} of {{n_origins}} origins, by R{{mean_gap}} on average and by at least R{{min_gap}}.
- The AR(1) forecast a rise at {{ar1_up_pct}} per cent of origins. On average its forecast sat {{ar1_mean_dev_pct}} per cent from spot.
- Its mean error (forecast minus outcome) was {{ar1_bias}}, against {{rw_bias}} for the random walk and {{combo_bias}} for the combination.
- Squared bias is {{ar1_bias_share_pct}} per cent of the AR(1)'s mean squared error and {{combo_bias_share_pct}} per cent of the combination's. The AR(1)'s mean squared error is {{mse_gap}} higher than the combination's, and {{bias_share_of_gap_pct}} per cent of that gap is the difference in squared bias. The remainder is variance.

These are observations. The AR(1) leans downward, and the difference in squared bias accounts for {{bias_share_of_gap_pct}} per cent of its excess squared error against the combination. I have not shown that this is the full explanation, or that it would persist. The rand's own path over {{first_origin}} to {{last_origin}} is one sample, and a mean-reverting model will do better in a sample where the rand reverts. The AR(1) was ahead in {{behind_year}}, and I have not tested why.

