## 10. Statistical inference and planning

### The Diebold-Mariano test

Forecasts four weeks ahead overlap, so the loss differential between two models is serially correlated by construction. Every DM statistic here uses a Newey-West long-run variance with a Bartlett kernel, at lag 3 (`h - 1`) as the primary choice. The statistic is the mean of `loss(model) - loss(benchmark)`, with squared error as the loss, divided by its standard error. A negative value favours the model. P-values are two-sided and use the normal distribution. The intervals are 95% intervals for the mean loss differential.

Two comparisons were fixed before the judged window was scored: the combination against the AR(1) and the combination against the random walk. They are reported with unadjusted p-values and at several lags. Everything else is exploratory.

| Comparison | DM (lag 3) | p | 95% interval, mean squared-error difference | RMSE difference | p across the four lags |
|:---|---:|---:|:---|---:|:---|
| Combination vs AR(1) | {{dm_combo_ar1}} | {{p_combo_ar1}} | {{ci_combo_ar1_lo}} to {{ci_combo_ar1_hi}} | {{rmse_diff_combo_ar1}} | {{p_combo_ar1_min}} to {{p_combo_ar1_max}} |
| Combination vs random walk | {{dm_combo_rw}} | {{p_combo_rw}} | {{ci_combo_rw_lo}} to {{ci_combo_rw_hi}} | {{rmse_diff_combo_rw}} | {{p_combo_rw_min}} to {{p_combo_rw_max}} |

The four lags are 3, 4, 8 and the Andrews (1991) automatic choice, which was {{auto_lag_ar1}} against the AR(1) and {{auto_lag_rw}} against the random walk. The answer does not depend on the lag. Both intervals contain zero. On the RMSE scale, holding the benchmark's MSE fixed, the interval for the combination against the AR(1) is {{rmse_ci_combo_ar1_lo}} to {{rmse_ci_combo_ar1_hi}} rand.

### Exploratory comparisons

Each non-prespecified model is compared with both benchmarks. That is {{n_exploratory}} distinct hypotheses (the random walk against the AR(1) counts once). The p-values are adjusted together by Holm's step-down method. {{n_exploratory_unadjusted}} of the {{n_exploratory}} have an unadjusted p-value below 0.05, and {{n_holm_significant}} survive the adjustment. The smallest adjusted p-value is {{min_holm_p}}.

{{table_dm}}

"n/a" marks the two prespecified comparisons, which are not adjusted.

### What the results can and cannot say

The combination moves almost in step with the random walk. Its forecast differs from spot by {{combo_mean_abs_dev_pct}} per cent on average. It forecast a rise at {{combo_up_pct}} per cent of origins, because carry's tilt is always upward, while the rand rose at {{realized_up_pct}} per cent of them. So its directional accuracy ({{combo_dir_acc_pct}} per cent) is just the share of origins at which the rand rose, and the combination carries no directional information.

### Planning table

An earlier version of this repository ran a power calculation at the observed effect size, which says nothing about what a study could detect. This table fixes the effect sizes in advance instead: RMSE reductions of 1, 2 and 5 per cent, chosen as round numbers and not as a statement of what the literature has achieved. It gives the number of weekly origins at which a two-sided 5% Diebold-Mariano test would detect each one with 80 per cent power.

