The two tables differ because the loss differential has a different standard deviation in each case ({{plan_ar1_sd}} against the AR(1), {{plan_rw_sd}} against the random walk). The requirements against the random walk are small only because the combination and the random walk are nearly the same forecast, which keeps that standard deviation small. A reduction of 1 to 5 per cent between two forecasts that differ from each other by {{combo_mean_abs_dev_pct}} per cent of spot is implausible, so those rows show what the test could detect if such an effect existed, and are no forecast of what a holdout would find.

The planning table is about this combination against these two benchmarks and says nothing about any other forecaster's results.

### Rolling accuracy

Over {{roll_windows}} complete {{roll_weeks}}-week rolling windows the combination had a lower RMSE than the AR(1) in {{roll_share}} per cent of them. The best window ratio (combination over AR(1)) was {{roll_best}} and the worst {{roll_worst}}. Adjacent windows share most of their origins, so this is one comparison viewed through a moving frame and not {{roll_windows}} independent results.

